# Frontends-Only Root Compose

The root `docker-compose.yml` is a **frontends-only orchestrator**. It defines
the application-plane services and *joins* the standalone grobase backend over an
**external Docker network** — it does not define the backend itself.

> [!info] Two Docker projects, one stack
> The frontends are one docker-compose project. **grobase** (project name
> `mini-baas`) is a separate docker project. They meet on a shared external
> network, not in one compose file.

## What is — and is not — a root service

> [!warning] These are NOT root-compose services
> Postgres, Kong, gotrue, postgrest, redis, pg-meta, vault, and mailpit are
> **not** defined in the root compose. They live in the grobase stack. The root
> stack only *reaches* them.

| In the root compose | In grobase (external) |
|:--------------------|:----------------------|
| opposite-osiris-web (Astro) | Kong (front door, `:8000`) |
| osionos (React + Vite) | postgrest · gotrue · redis |
| osionos-bridge (TS) | postgres · pg-meta · vault |
| auth-gateway (Go) | realtime (Rust) · mailpit |
| mail / calendar (+ bridges) | |
| local-https-proxy · livekit | |

## The TLS front door

A single **`local-https-proxy`** service is the TLS front door. It publishes the
8 primary frontend host ports behind HTTPS. Two services publish their own ports
instead of going through the proxy: **livekit** (media), and
**opposite-osiris-web** (its debug `4323 → 8080`).

```text
   Browser ── HTTPS ──► local-https-proxy
                          ├─► opposite-osiris-web   :4322
                          ├─► osionos               :3001
                          ├─► osionos-bridge        :4000
                          ├─► auth-gateway          :8787
                          ├─► mail / mail-bridge     :3002 / :4100
                          └─► calendar / cal-bridge  :3003 / :4200
```

## Self-provisioning startup

The whole stack comes up from one command. `make all` is **self-provisioning**:
from a clean clone it syncs submodules, ensures secrets, regenerates TLS certs,
**starts grobase**, restores demo data when the databases are empty, then builds
the frontends with health validation.

- [x] `make all` starts the backend if it is down
- [x] certs regenerate on every `make all` (intentional)
- [x] demo data restores only when engines are confirmed empty (never wipes)

> [!danger] Profile gotcha
> `COMPOSE_PROFILES` defaults to `dev`. A *manual* `docker compose up` without it
> silently skips most frontends. The Makefile names services explicitly, so
> `make all` is unaffected — but raw compose commands are a trap.

## Two host-network simulations

Beyond the eight frontends, two host-network simulation services also exist:
`playground-simulation` and `agency-simulation`. They are org/tenant simulations,
not part of the primary user path.

> [!tip] The wiring itself
> How the frontends *address* grobase — by container-name DNS — is its own topic:
> the [container-DNS topology](container-dns-topology.xmd).
